1. AN ASCII FILE ALREADY IS A UTF-8 FILE
   "Hello".encode("ascii") == "Hello".encode("utf-8") -> True
   Not a conversion, not a compatibility mode -- the same bytes. Every
   ASCII document ever written was already valid UTF-8 on the day the
   encoding was designed, which is what made adoption free.

2. NO FRAGMENT OF A CHARACTER CAN LOOK LIKE ASCII
   every byte of every non-ASCII character here: all >= 0x80
   So a byte below 0x80 is always that ASCII character and never part of
   something else. grep for a slash and you cannot match half a Chinese
   character -- which is exactly the bug overlong sequences reintroduce.

3. YOU CAN START ANYWHERE
   bytes 41 c3 a9 c5 bc e4 b8 ad f0 9f 98 80
   lead bytes at offsets [0, 1, 3, 5, 8]
   A continuation byte is 10xxxxxx and a lead byte never is, so from any
   offset you can walk backwards at most three bytes to find the start
   of a character. UTF-16 has no such rule mid-stream, and a code page
   with shift states has none at all.

4. A WRONG GUESS FAILS LOUDLY
   b'\xe9\xe9\xe9'.decode('utf-8') -> UnicodeDecodeError
   the same bytes as latin-1      -> '\\xe9\\xe9\\xe9'
   Latin-1 accepts every byte, so a wrong guess there is silent forever.
   UTF-8's structure means most wrong guesses produce invalid sequences
   quickly -- which is the property that makes detection possible at all.

5. THERE IS NO BYTE ORDER TO GET WRONG
   utf-8    41 c3 a9
   utf-16le 41 00 e9 00
   utf-16be 00 41 00 e9
   The unit is one byte, so there is nothing to order. No BOM is needed,
   and the three bytes some tools write are a signature, not a mark.

6. SORTING THE BYTES SORTS THE CHARACTERS
   by code point ['A', 'é', 'ż', '中', '😀']
   by utf-8 byte ['A', 'é', 'ż', '中', '😀']
   same order? True
   True for UTF-8 and NOT for UTF-16, where the surrogate range sits
   below U+FFFF numerically but encodes characters above it -- so a
   UTF-16 byte sort puts every emoji in the middle of the BMP.

WHAT IT PAYS
   English  utf-8  11 bytes   utf-16  22 bytes   utf-8 wins
   Chinese  utf-8  18 bytes   utf-16  12 bytes   UTF-16 IS SMALLER
   CJK text costs 3 bytes per character in UTF-8 and 2 in UTF-16, so
   the bill is real and it is paid by the people who write in those
   scripts. That argument lost -- to the six properties above, and to
   the fact that most bytes on the wire are markup, which is ASCII.
